## 2026-08-31 architecture decision-log entry (targeted architecture-correction and scoping pass, pre-Phase-17 -- LLD 7.4/9.1's false "weighted, sticky traffic split" premise; new ADR-0019 + new LLD section 15)

**Trigger**: before dispatching Phase 17 (BL-48, Progressive rollout), the orchestrating session found that the phase's premise -- "traffic-split canary on channel-to-version binding" -- rests on functionality that does not exist. This dispatch confirmed and EXTENDED that finding, decided the real buildable scope, and wrote it down. **No code was written; Phase 17 was not started.** This is the THIRD such correction pass this initiative has run (after the capability-group HLD/LLD conflict and ADR-0013 section 7's `apps/runtime`/BullMQ premise), and it is precisely the "separate, bounded doc-reconciliation dispatch" that the ADR-0013 correction pass's own closing "open item for the orchestrator" recommended for the wider LLD section 7.4 doc-vs-reality gap.

**Investigation -- every claim checked against the working tree, and TWO findings beyond the orchestrator's own brief**: (1) LLD 7.4 step 4 and LLD 9.1's sequence line both describe a weighted/sticky Deployment traffic-split resolver; the live path is `apps/gateway/src/lib/turn-pipeline-adapter.ts:115`, `findActiveAgentDefinitionVersion(ctx)` -- tenant-wide, most recently promoted `Production` version, no channel/agent-definition/weight/stickiness parameter of any kind. (2) `channel.agent_definition_version_id` (migration `0010_channels.sql:19`) is read and written by **nothing** -- confirmed by a repo-wide grep whose only non-`.next`-build-artifact hit is a test comment saying exactly that ("nothing ever set `channel.agent_definition_version_id`"); zero references in `packages/modules/channels`. It is vestigial, and its inline "that table doesn't exist until Phase 10" comment is stale by many phases. (3) `deployment`'s schema and its `SUM(traffic_split_pct)=100` trigger are real, but `createInitialProductionDeployment` and `emergencyRollbackRepoint` are the only writers and both deactivate-all-then-insert-one-100%-row; `SplitChange`/`PromoteCanary` have existed in the `deployment_action` enum, unwritten, since migration `0014`. (4) **Additional finding not in the brief**: there is no Deployments/Canary surface at all -- no `/deployments` console route, no `GET/PUT /api/v1/admin/deployments`, no `promote-canary` or `rollback` endpoint. LLD 5.9 lists all three as design intent only. (5) **Second additional finding, and the one that most changes the scope**: even ignoring channels entirely, **there is currently no way for a live turn to know which agent definition should answer it.** `findActiveAgentDefinitionVersion` takes only a `TenantContext`; a traffic split is keyed on `agent_definition_id`; so the missing routing hop -- not the split arithmetic -- is the real gap. (6) Confirmed sound and untouched: `emergencyRollbackRepoint` (ADR-0017, Phase 0) and the Phase 6 trace tree. Also confirmed `emergencyRollbackRepoint`'s `UPDATE ... WHERE is_active = true` already matches N rows, so it collapses a multi-row canary correctly **with no code change**.

**DECISION 1 -- the canary binds at `(tenant, agent_definition, environment)`, NOT per channel.** BL-48's "channel-to-version binding" phrasing is the outlier in this project's own documents and is rejected as the binding unit: FR-AGT-04's own text says "per agent/environment"; the shipped `deployment` table, its `SUM=100` trigger, and the `pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext("<tenant>:<agent>:<env>"))` key that serializes promotion **and** emergency rollback are all keyed on exactly that triple. Binding a canary per channel would make the shipped invariant meaningless and desynchronize emergency rollback's lock key from the thing it must roll back.

**DECISION 2 -- but the missing channel to agent-definition hop IS in scope**, because it is what BL-48's phrasing was really groping at and the resolver structurally requires it. `channel.agent_definition_version_id` is dropped; `channel.agent_definition_id` is added, with a one-definition-per-tenant backfill and a NULL-binding fallback to today's tenant-wide lookup (retained, renamed, not deleted) so nothing regresses. "channel to version binding" is thus delivered as two correctly-factored halves: channel -> agent definition (which bot), deployment split -> version (which build).

**DECISION 3 -- the resolver**: deterministic weighted selection (`sha256(conversationId + ':' + agentDefinitionId) mod 10000` walked over active rows ordered by `deployment.id`), sticky via a new `deployment_traffic_assignment` table, and -- the load-bearing rule -- **stickiness is bounded by the deployment's own `is_active` lifetime**: an assignment whose deployment was deactivated is discarded and re-resolved. This is what keeps ADR-0017 true; a stickiness that survived deactivation would make canary rollback silently ineffective for exactly the in-flight conversations being harmed. Also decided: **no Redis routing cache** (LLD 3.10's 5s-TTL cache is deferred -- a 5s TTL is in direct tension with NFR-2's 5s rollback bound).

**DECISION 4 -- how much of BL-13 Phase 17 must build**: `setTrafficSplit`/`promoteCanary` (the first multi-row-active writers, on the SAME advisory-lock key, one `deployment_history` `SplitChange`/`PromoteCanary` row plus a same-transaction `domain_event` outbox row), the resolver, the channel binding, and a Deployments and Canary panel on the existing agent-definition detail screen with per-version live metrics from `agent_run`. **A version must already hold `Production` status before receiving any canary traffic** -- canary is not a second route past the promotion gate, so Phase 17 introduces no new gate-bypass surface. Explicitly OUT: a separate top-level Deployment Manager console area, any new rollback action (Phase 0's already works on N rows; Phase 17 adds the test, not code), metric-triggered auto-promotion, and the Redis cache.

**DECISION 5 -- how shadow evaluation actually works, given real turn-pipeline side effects**: **asynchronous replay**, not synchronous dual-run. Synchronous was rejected outright (a second model round trip inside the customer's request doubles NFR-2 latency exposure, consumes two `tenant_runtime_quota` slots per turn so an experiment can 429 real customers, and makes a shadow bug a live-path bug). Instead: a `shadow_run` durable work table drained by new `apps/worker` jobs (`deployment.shadow-run-pump` 5s / `shadow-lease-reaper` 60s) using the `knowledge_ingestion_job` claim/lease idiom ADR-0013 section 7 established. The replay reuses the **real** `runTurnPipeline` under a new additive `executionMode: "Shadow"`. Four containments, each structural rather than by convention: (a) **no real tool execution** -- the worker injects a non-executing `createShadowEgressPort()`, and because `EgressPort` is the only route out of `orchestration` (ADR-0004's choke point, already lint-enforced), a shadow turn *physically cannot* reach an MCP server; a read-only/dry-run tool mode was considered and REJECTED because it would require correctly classifying every tool in every tenant's registry (including third-party MCP servers) as read-only forever, and one misclassification is a real write against a real customer system; (b) **no approval-queue pollution** -- a new `ShadowSuppressed` tier outcome records the tier that *would* have been required and creates no `tool_call`/`approval_request` (deliberately distinct from the existing `PolicyDenied{no_conversation_context}` shortcut, which would hide the finding); (c) **no customer exposure** -- automatic by call site (the worker never inserts a message, publishes SSE, or calls `triggerEscalation`; the escalation signal is captured as data), plus guardrail-event writes suppressed so the Guardrail screen is not polluted; (d) **no analytics corruption** -- `agent_run.trigger` gains `ShadowEvaluation` and Phase 17 must exhaustively audit every existing `agent_run` reader to exclude it (named as the phase's single highest-risk item). Shadow runs store **pointers, not a transcript copy** (no new PII sink / DSR-retention target); a purged source conversation yields `Skipped(SourceGone)`. Shadow spend is real, charged through the Model Gateway with unchanged residency governance, capped by `sample_pct`/`max_runs`/`max_cost_usd`, and reported separately. Shadow results are evidence, never a gate: promotion is unchanged.

