**What was built**: `tenant_breakglass_grant` (new tenant-scoped, RLS'd table -- migrations 0090/0091 -- the tenant's own explicit, time-boxed, revocable consent grant; no other mechanism grants operator access). Tenant-side application (`packages/modules/tenancy/src/application/breakglass-grant.ts` + `domain/breakglass-grant-policy.ts`): create (enforces one-active-grant-per-tenant via `BreakglassGrantAlreadyActiveError` 409, and a platform-enforced 24h time-box cap via `BreakglassGrantExpiryTooLongError` 422, never silently clamped), revoke (idempotent), get-active, get-most-recent (for audit-denial-reason classification only), list -- create/revoke each write a `domain_event` (`breakglass.grant_created`/`breakglass.grant_revoked`), the tenant-side half of "doubly-audited", mirrored into `audit_log_entry` by `@nextbot/audit`'s existing, unmodified outbox sync job (never written directly, per that module's own boundary rule). Platform-ops application (`packages/modules/tenancy/src/application/breakglass-access.ts`, the ONLY new code constructing a `TenantContext` from a bare tenant id on an operator's behalf, since `packages/modules/tenancy` is one of only two allowed `withPlatform()` callers): `activateBreakglassAccess` is the one auditable lifecycle event a diagnosis session produces -- on success writes BOTH a `platform_audit_log_entry` row (operator trail, `withPlatform`) and a real tenant-side `domain_event` (`withTenant`, correctly attributed to the operator's actor label); on denial (no grant/revoked/expired/tenant not found) writes only the platform-side row, fail-closed regardless of the operator's own role/token validity -- this is the spec's own named hard requirement. `requireActiveBreakglassTenantContext` is the separate, unaudited, always-re-validated gate every read-diagnosis endpoint calls, so a mid-session revocation takes effect on the very next read without needing a background sweep (mirrors `auth_session`/`scim_token`'s own synchronous `revoked_at`/`expires_at` check-at-use-time convention -- no new scheduled job).

**Tenant-side surface**: `apps/web/app/api/v1/admin/breakglass-grants/**` (list/create/revoke, RBAC `security_settings`) + `/settings/breakglass-access` screen + a Settings hub card. **Platform-ops surface**: `apps/web/app/api/internal/ops/tenants/[id]/breakglass/{status,activate,conversations,conversations/[conversationId],escalations,escalations/[escalationId]}/route.ts`, every route gated by the unmodified `requirePlatformApi()` (token + IP allowlist + rate limit, identical to every existing `internal/ops` route) plus the separate grant-validity gate; a minimal Platform Manager console screen (linked from Tenant Detail) reusing the console's established Card/Badge/AlertDialog conventions.

**The read-only diagnosis path is a structural masking-parity guarantee, not a re-implementation**: `listConversationsForAdmin`/`getConversationDetailForAdmin` (`@nextbot/conversations`) and `listEscalationsForAdmin`/`getEscalationDetail` (`@nextbot/escalations`) are called **verbatim** from the ops composition root with a `TenantContext` built from the grant -- the exact same functions the tenant admin's own screens call -- so "an operator sees no more than an equivalent-privilege tenant viewer would" holds by construction. Proven by a real integration test asserting the two routes return a byte-identical `conversation` payload for the same conversation. Deliberately narrower than the tenant-facing `escalations/[id]` route: no `@nextbot/orchestration` tool-call (`aiAttempts`) join, disclosed as a bounded-scope narrowing, not a silent gap.

**Six disclosed design decisions** (full rationale in the plan doc): (1) one active grant per tenant at a time; (2) 24h platform-enforced max time-box, rejected not clamped; (3) audit granularity is lifecycle-events-only (grant created/revoked/access activated/access denied), not per-individual-read -- every read still independently re-validates grant activity at call time; (4) the tenant-side `domain_event` write and the platform-side `platform_audit_log_entry` write are two different Postgres roles/connections and are not atomically joined (same limitation `provisionTenant()`'s own audit write already lives with) -- a crash between them can leave one trail short an entry but can never cause an invalid grant to be treated as valid, since the validity check happens before either write; (5) no `aiAttempts` join on the break-glass escalation-detail read; (6) denied activation attempts are audited platform-side only, never tenant-side (nothing happened to the tenant's data).

**Metadata-only rollup untouched**: `health-rollup/route.ts` was not touched by this phase at all; its own pre-existing, unmodified regression tests (byte-exact response shape via `toEqual`, plus a dedicated "never includes a conversation/message-shaped key" guard) were re-run as part of this dispatch's full unit suite and confirmed still green -- this phase's own proof that the rollup's scope was never relaxed.

