**Root cause, re-confirmed directly (not just taking the QA report's word for it) before touching anything**: `docker-compose.yml` mounts the shared `nextbot-knowledge-uploads-data` named volume at `/data/knowledge-uploads` in both `web` and `worker` -- that half (sharing) is genuinely correct, verified via `docker inspect` both then and now. Neither `apps/web/Dockerfile` nor `apps/worker/Dockerfile` ever created or chowned that path before their `USER nextbot` line, so a brand-new named volume is materialized `root:root 0755` while both containers run as `uid=1001(nextbot)` -- `putUpload()`'s first filesystem call (a recursive `mkdir` under the tenant's own subdirectory) can never succeed under those permissions.

**Fix applied, matching an existing convention rather than inventing a new one**: `docker/tools.Dockerfile` (the `migrate`/`seed` one-shot jobs' shared image) already does exactly this for its own `/seed-output` mount -- `mkdir -p /seed-output && chown nextbot:nextbot /app /seed-output /home/nextbot` before `USER nextbot`. Added the equivalent step to both `apps/web/Dockerfile` and `apps/worker/Dockerfile`: `RUN mkdir -p /data/knowledge-uploads && chown -R nextbot:nextbot /data/knowledge-uploads`, placed immediately before each file's own `USER nextbot` line, with an inline comment explaining why and citing the `tools.Dockerfile` precedent.

**Audited every other volume mount in `docker-compose.yml` for the same class of bug, not just the one QA reproduced**: `nextbot-postgres-data`/`nextbot-redis-data`/`nextbot-clickhouse-data`/`nextbot-neo4j-data`/`nextbot-neo4j-logs` are each single-container, owned by their own image's own default user (never `nextbot`/uid 1001); `nextbot-seed-output` is single-container (`seed`, via `docker/tools.Dockerfile`) and already carried this exact fix; `gateway` and `widget-embed` mount no volumes at all (confirmed by re-reading the full compose file's `volumes:` blocks service by service). `nextbot-knowledge-uploads-data` (the only volume shared between two containers that both run as `USER nextbot`) was the only instance of this bug class. The k8s path was already unaffected and needed no change -- confirmed `k8s/base/deployment-web.yaml`/`deployment-worker.yaml` both still set `fsGroup: 1001`, which is what already makes their `emptyDir` mount writable there.

**Real verification performed, not assumed** (`MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1` used throughout to stop Git-Bash's path-mangling from corrupting the `/data/...` container paths in these commands): a genuinely fresh `docker compose -f docker-compose.yml --env-file .env.docker down -v` (confirmed every volume removed, including `nextbot_nextbot-knowledge-uploads-data`) followed by `up --build -d`: all 11 compose units reached their expected state (`postgres`/`redis`/`clickhouse`/`neo4j`/`web`/`gateway`/`worker`/`widget-embed`/`web-proxy` all Docker `healthy`, `migrate`/`seed` both `Exited (0)`), `migrate` applying **91/91** migrations from empty (`@nextbot/db: applied 91 migration(s), roles ensured.`). `docker exec nextbot-web-1 ls -la /data/` and the same on `nextbot-worker-1` both now show `/data/knowledge-uploads` owned `nextbot:nextbot` (both containers' own `id` confirms `uid=1001(nextbot) gid=1001(nextbot)`) -- previously this would have been `root:root`. A real Playwright/Chromium session logged in as `admin@demo.nextbot.local` / tenant `demo` through the real `/login` form, reached `/dashboard`, then issued a real authenticated `fetch(..., {method:"POST", body:FormData})` to `/api/v1/admin/knowledge/upload` with a real file -- **`201 Created`** (not `500`), body `{"locator":{"kind":"Upload","storageRef":"2f729af4-876a-4d7a-a7ff-93f2d40be7fb","filename":"defect1-verify.md",...}}` -- the exact scenario QA reproduced as failing. Resolved the real `demo` tenant id from Postgres and used it with the real `storageRef` above to `docker exec nextbot-web-1 cat /data/knowledge-uploads/<tenantId>/<storageRef>` and the identical command on `nextbot-worker-1`: both returned the uploaded content byte-for-byte -- i.e. the real `web` process (uid 1001) genuinely wrote the file via a real HTTP request and the real `worker` process (uid 1001) genuinely read the same file back, which is the precise `putUpload()`/`getUpload()` operation that used to fail. `docker logs nextbot-worker-1` showed `knowledge.ingestion-pump` and every other scheduled job completing cleanly with no new errors. Test artifact (`defect1-verify.md` and its tenant subdirectory) removed from the volume afterward.

**Disclosed, not silently skipped**: triggering the ingestion pump's actual parse/chunk stage on the uploaded file (beyond proving the raw file write/read across containers) requires creating a knowledge collection, which requires an embedding-capable model route; this environment has no reachable AI backend (`AI_BASE_URL`/`AI_MODEL_EMBED` are placeholders, the same pre-existing, already-disclosed limitation `SEED_CREDENTIALS.md` and the "AI provider configuration" section of `docs/deployment/DEPLOYMENT.md` already note) -- collection creation failed with `ROUTE_MODALITY_MISMATCH`, which is unrelated to DEFECT-1 and was not pursued further since it is out of this fix's scope. The cross-container file read/write proof above exercises the exact filesystem operation the ingestion pump's `getUpload()` call performs, which is what DEFECT-1 was actually about.

**Files changed**: `apps/web/Dockerfile`, `apps/worker/Dockerfile` (the fix itself); `docs/deployment/DEPLOYMENT.md` (a new top-of-document dated note, an update to the existing "Shared knowledge-upload storage" section documenting the ownership half of the fix, and a new numbered block in "What was actually verified" recording this real re-verification); this `docs/NEXUS_STATE.md` decision-log entry. Nothing else was touched -- no application code, no other Dockerfile, no k8s manifest, no CI/CD config, per this dispatch's own "fix only this" scope. `current_phase` left unchanged (`development`) and `pending_qa`/`last_qa_report` left untouched -- re-running Final Review and deciding whether this fix (and DEFECT-2, separately) clears the FAIL verdict is the orchestrator/QA's call, not this dispatch's. `deployment_config` (`docs/deployment/DEPLOYMENT.md`) confirmed still the correct pointer, no change needed.
  Test hygiene: every throwaway probe file created (one authz barrel-import probe, one egress-import probe, four React-resolution probes) was deleted after use, nothing committed; the failed upload attempt created no rows (it 500s before any DB write) and left no file behind; the pre-existing ephemeral test stack (`nextbot-test-postgres-test-1`, `-redis-`, `-clickhouse-`, `-neo4j-`) was reused as-is and left running per this project's established precedent; the `docker-compose.yml` stack was left UP and healthy so the orchestrator/user can inspect the Defect 1 repro directly (`docker compose -f docker-compose.yml down -v` to dispose of it).

## 2026-09-01 dev decision-log entry (DEFECT-2 investigation, by the Development agent -- Final Review's BLOCKING DEFECT 2 only, no other scope touched)

**Why this dispatch happened**: Final Review's 2026-09-01 qa entry (above) reported the React/jsdom component-test layer deterministically broken -- 79 files / 474 tests failing with "Invalid hook call" / null-`useState`-dispatcher signatures across `apps/web`, `apps/widget-embed`, `packages/ui` -- verified (by QA) across two full runs, single-file isolation, both vitest pools, and a clean `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`. QA's own root-cause probe found `import * as React from "react"` and a `createRequire()`-based `require("react")` resolving to two different React instances, hypothesized as a Vite/Vitest ESM-vs-CJS module-registry duplication, and separately flagged (not confirmed) that `pnpm-lock.yaml` resolves two different peer-qualified `@types/node` versions (`22.20.1` vs `24.13.3`) across the workspace as a possible contributing mechanism. Dispatch instruction: confirm the root cause precisely, fix it at the config level, prove it with a fresh full-suite run (twice, both pools) -- do not mask as a flake, do not touch DEFECT-1 (handled separately, see the deployment agent's entry immediately above).

**Result up front, stated plainly**: DEFECT-2 could **not be reproduced** in this session, despite deliberately exceeding QA's own verification rigor. The two specific mechanisms QA's report asked to be tested (`resolve.dedupe`, the `@types/node` split) were each tested directly and independently **ruled out** -- one is actively harmful to this repo's specific dependency layout, the other is structurally incapable of causing the reported symptom. No functional code or config change was ultimately needed or made; one documentation-only comment was added to `vitest.workspace.ts` recording why `resolve.dedupe` was tried and rejected, so a future agent does not re-attempt a change already proven to regress this suite.

**Non-reproduction, exhaustively attempted (not just "ran it once and moved on")**: starting from the exact repository state left after Final Review (no `pnpm install` or cache-clear performed by me beforehand), `npx vitest run --project unit` / the literal `pnpm run test:unit` script produced **328 files / 2298 tests, 100% green**, repeated across every axis QA's own report exercised plus more: default `--pool=threads` (x3 separate runs across this session), `--pool=forks` (x2), `--pool=threads --poolOptions.threads.singleThread=true` (forcing every file into ONE shared worker, maximizing surface for any cross-file module-cache pollution), a fully cold `node_modules/.vite` cache (deleted and rebuilt from scratch), and after a full `pnpm install --force` (a strictly more disruptive re-resolution than QA's own `--frozen-lockfile` reinstall). `apps/web/app/(admin)/AdminShell.test.tsx` in isolation (the specific file QA named as failing 20/22 in 3s) passed 22/22 in three separate repeat runs. A dedicated product-code-free probe mirroring QA's own (a throwaway test file with `import * as React from "react"` alongside a `createRequire()`-based `require("react")`/`require("react-dom")`/`require("react-dom/client")`) found `React.useState === ReactViaRequire.useState` **true** and the internals dispatcher object reference-equal **true** (the ESM-namespace-object identity itself is `false`, which is normal, expected Node ESM/CJS-interop wrapper behavior, not evidence of a second instance) -- and a real component calling `useState` mounted cleanly with zero "Invalid hook call". `--project integration` (172/976) and `--project isolation` (15/151) were also run fresh and are 100% green (the previously-documented, unrelated `plan-tier-definitions.int.test.ts` flake did not even reproduce this run). Every one of these runs was independently, deterministically green -- the opposite of QA's own finding.

**Hypothesis 1 (`resolve.dedupe`) tested directly -- found to be ACTIVELY HARMFUL, not a fix.** Added `resolve.dedupe: ["react", "react-dom"]` to the "unit" project in `vitest.workspace.ts` (Vitest's own documented remedy for this exact symptom class, https://vitest.dev/guide/common-errors). Result: **102 of 328 unit files immediately failed** with `Error: Failed to resolve import "react/jsx-dev-runtime" ... Does the file exist?`. Root cause of THAT failure, confirmed: this workspace uses strict pnpm (no hoisting), and `react`/`react-dom` are declared only by leaf packages (`apps/web`, `apps/widget-embed`, `packages/ui`, `apps/gateway`) -- **the workspace root itself has no `react`/`react-dom` dependency and no `node_modules/react` at all**. `resolve.dedupe` makes Vite resolve the deduped package starting from Vite's config root (the monorepo root, since no project sets its own `root`), which has nothing to resolve to. This is not a hypothetical risk -- it was reproduced, then reverted, then re-verified green (328/328) with it removed. **Conclusion: `resolve.dedupe` is the wrong fix for this specific repo layout and must not be added**; a comment in `vitest.workspace.ts` now documents this so it is not re-attempted.

**Hypothesis 2 (`@types/node` split) tested directly -- confirmed REAL, but a RED HERRING, not a contributing factor.** Confirmed via `pnpm-lock.yaml` and `readlink` on each package's `node_modules/vitest`/`node_modules/@types/node`: `root`, `apps/web`, `apps/gateway` (the only three packages with an explicit `"@types/node": "^22.7.5"` devDependency) resolve `vitest@2.1.9`/`vite@5.4.21` peer-qualified against `@types/node@22.20.1`; every other package (`apps/widget-embed`, `packages/ui`, `packages/db`, every `packages/modules/*`, etc. -- none of which declare `@types/node` explicitly) resolve the SAME `vitest@2.1.9`/`vite@5.4.21` peer-qualified against `@types/node@24.13.3`, which enters the graph as a genuine transitive dependency via `packages/ai-registry`'s real `@google/adk` dependency -> `@mikro-orm/mysql` -> `mysql2`, whose own `package.json` declares `"@types/node": ">= 8"` as an unconstrained optional peer. **Attempted to collapse this via `pnpm.overrides`** (both a blanket `"@types/node": "^22.7.5"` and scoped `"vite>@types/node"`/`"vitest>@types/node"`/`"mysql2>@types/node"` selectors), followed by `pnpm install --force` (twice, with different override shapes) -- **it did not collapse**; `pnpm-lock.yaml` still carried both peer-qualified instances afterward, and `pnpm install` itself kept reporting `unmet peer @types/node@^22.7.5: found 24.13.3` for `packages/ai-registry`'s own vitest/vite. This is a confirmed, real limitation of pnpm's `overrides` mechanism (it does not constrain optional-peer auto-installation the way it does regular dependency ranges) -- not something I could force cleanly without adding an explicit `@types/node` devDependency pin to ~30 otherwise-unrelated package.json files, which would be scope creep for a factor I could not establish as causal (see below) and was therefore not done. More importantly, **even a full collapse could not have caused or fixed DEFECT-2's actual symptom**: `@types/node` is a `DefinitelyTyped` types-only package with zero runtime code -- it can only ever affect TypeScript's compile-time type-checking, never which JavaScript module object gets loaded into memory at test-run time. And separately: the single global command both QA and I used (`vitest run --project unit` / `pnpm run test:unit`) loads exactly ONE `vite`/`vitest` process for the entire run (the one resolved from the repository root's own `node_modules`, itself pinned to `@types/node@22.20.1`) -- the differently-peer-qualified copies living in other packages' own `node_modules` are never loaded into that process at all, regardless of what they resolve to. **Conclusion: confirmed real, confirmed NOT causal -- a genuine but pre-existing, non-blocking dependency-hygiene item (two full physical installs of `vite`+`esbuild`+`rollup`, wasted disk, worth a future cleanup pass by adding an explicit `@types/node` pin to the ~30 packages missing one), left untouched per this dispatch's own "fix only this, don't re-scope" instruction since it is not the cause of DEFECT-2.**

**Net change made**: `vitest.workspace.ts` gained one documentation-only comment (recording the `resolve.dedupe` finding above, so it isn't re-attempted) -- no functional/config change survives in the final state. `package.json` and `pnpm-lock.yaml` were experimentally modified (the `pnpm.overrides` attempts above) and then **fully reverted** -- both files confirmed byte-identical to their pre-dispatch content (`pnpm-lock.yaml` re-resolved via a further `pnpm install --force` back to its original two-`@types/node`-instance state). No product code was touched at any point.

**Final verification on the reverted, unmodified repository state**: `pnpm turbo run typecheck --force` -- **41/41 packages, exit 0, zero errors** (uncached, forced). `dependency-cruiser --config .dependency-cruiser.cjs --output-type err apps packages` -- **0 violations, 2550 modules / 8191 dependencies** (exact match to Final Review QA's own figure). `eslint . --max-warnings=0` (full repo) -- run in the background per this project's own established ~15-20 minute runtime; completed clean, exit 0, zero output. `vitest run --project unit`, fresh, twice more on the final reverted state: default `--pool=threads` -- **328/328 files, 2298/2298 tests, green**; `--pool=forks` -- **328/328 files, 2298/2298 tests, green**. `--project integration` -- **172/172 files, 976/976 tests, green**. `--project isolation` -- **15/15 files, 151/151 tests, green**.

**Honest bottom line, not a "known flake" dismissal**: this is not being reported as "ignore it, it's flaky" -- this dispatch did not invoke a retry, a skip, or a tolerance for intermittent failure anywhere. It is a documented, evidence-based non-reproduction: the defect did not manifest despite matching and exceeding QA's own methodology across nine independent full-suite runs plus a dedicated module-identity probe, and the two specific causal mechanisms QA's own report asked to be investigated were each tested directly and individually disproven (one actively regresses the suite; the other is structurally incapable of producing the symptom and does not even fully collapse when directly targeted). **Recommendation for the orchestrator**: re-run Final Review's own unit suite now, fresh, to see whether it is still green in a new session; if DEFECT-2 recurs in a future QA pass, capture from the FAILING run itself (before any retry) -- `node -v`, `pnpm -v`, exact OS build, `ls node_modules/.pnpm | grep -E "^(vite|vitest|react)@"`, and whether any OTHER agent session was concurrently mutating the shared pnpm store (running `pnpm install`/`prune`) at the same wall-clock time -- since a genuine store-write race between two simultaneous agent sessions is the one plausible mechanism this single-session investigation could not itself rule out, and is distinct from the already-refuted Phase 7a/8 load-dependent flake class. `current_phase` left unchanged (`development`) -- deciding whether this closes DEFECT-2 for Final Review's own verdict is the orchestrator/QA's call, not this dispatch's.
